<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eucratides I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 170–145 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Eucratides the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eucratides I was a Greco-Bactrian king who seized power around 170 BCE, overthrowing the Euthydemid dynasty and fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> kings south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>. He issued some of the finest Hellenistic coins, including the largest gold coin of antiquity, a twenty-stater piece. He was murdered around 145 BCE, reportedly by his own son, and soon afterwards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> fell to nomad invaders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eucratides-i.jpg" alt="Eucratides I" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eucratides I / BnF · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eucratides_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eucratides was probably born around 210 to 205 BCE, the son of Heliocles and Laodice, as shown on his coinage. His mother wears a royal diadem on the coins, which points to royal descent, while his father is bare-headed. It is unclear whether he was a Bactrian nobleman who raised a rebellion or, according to some scholars, a cousin of the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a> trying to regain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>. Tarn held that Laodice was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-ii-callinicus">Seleucus II Callinicus</a>, a view that Narain and other modern authors have challenged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Eucratides took power by overthrowing the Euthydemid dynasty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, possibly while Demetrius I was conquering north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The king he dethroned was probably Antimachus I. The Roman historian Justin places his accession at about the time Mithridates began to rule the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, which is known to be 171 BCE. Mithridates I took Tapuria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a> from Demetrius around 170 BCE and then tried to conquer Bactria itself, but Eucratides checked him.</p><p>With his western border secured, Eucratides conquered parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, campaigning as far south as Barigaza, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a>. Justin says he was besieged for four months by Demetrius, king of the Indians, and that he defeated 60,000 enemies with 300 soldiers; which Demetrius is meant is uncertain. His bilingual coins are found widely across northern India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. The city of Eucratideia on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> was probably named for him, and may have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a> or Dilbarjin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Justin reports that Eucratides was murdered on his way back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by his son, who drove a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> over his blood and left the corpse unburied. This may be a misreading, and the killer may have been the Euthydemid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-nicator">Demetrius II</a>, since patricide was uncommon in the Hellenistic age. A civil war among the dynasty's members probably followed. His successors were Eucratides II and Heliocles I, the last Greek king to rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, and the Greco-Bactrians lost the lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a>, his capital, was likely destroyed at the end of his reign, about 145 BCE. The seal of Da Afghanistan Bank, the central bank of Afghanistan, bears one of his coins with the Greek legend of the great king Eucratides.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eucratides I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Eucratides I</b>, either a general of Demetrius or an ally of the Seleucids, overthrew the Euthydemids around 170 BC, probably dethroning Antimachus I and Antimachus II.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
